      "[Desired outcome] without [important frustration].",
      "Or:",
      "[Product or service] for [specific customer] who wants [specific result].",
      "These are drafting structures, not mandatory formulas. The final wording should reflect the actual offer and the customer's language.\n4.3 Put credible social proof where visitors can see it\nThe source identifies social proof as a core conversion element. Reviews, customer photographs, and specific testimonials can reduce uncertainty because visitors often trust other customers more than a brand's own claims.",
      "Useful forms of proof include:\n- Verified customer reviews.\n- Customer-submitted photographs or videos, with permission.\n- Specific testimonials describing a real experience.\n- Ratings with a clear and accurate review count.\n- Case studies or documented results.\n- Recognised certifications, if genuinely held.\nDo not fabricate reviews, customer names, photographs, sales totals, review counts, or “verified buyer” labels. The demonstration itself jokes about invented review counts and customer totals. Those moments should be treated as warnings, not as tactics to copy.",
      "If a business is new and has no reviews, it should say so honestly. It can instead provide clear product demonstrations, transparent specifications, founder information, policies, and early feedback collected from genuine testers.\n4.4 Make the customer recognise themselves\nThe page should feel relevant to the intended customer. This applies to:\n- Photography.\n- Models and settings.\n- Colours and typography.\n- Vocabulary and tone.\n- Product demonstrations.\n- Benefits emphasised.\n- Questions answered.\nThe source describes the first generated page as too generic and visually disconnected from the intended buyer. It then uses branding to make the website feel more aligned with the customer.",
      "Do not assume that a design is effective because it looks attractive to the founder. Ask whether the target customer would recognise their own situation and understand the value.\n4.5 Build the rest of the page around the decision\nA useful product landing page may include:\n1. Hero section: Clear offer, headline, supporting line, primary call to action.\n2. Problem and outcome: Show the situation the customer wants to improve.\n3. Product explanation: Explain how the product works.\n4. Benefits and features: Connect features to customer outcomes.\n5. Product visuals: Use accurate images and demonstrations.\n6. Social proof: Display genuine reviews or other credible evidence.\n7. Offer details: Show price, variants, bundles, and what is included.\n8. Risk reduction: Explain shipping, returns, warranties, and guarantees accurately.\n9. FAQs: Answer real objections and compatibility questions.\n10. Final call to action: Make the next step obvious.\nThe order can change depending on the product, price, audience, and complexity. The page should answer the customer's questions in the order they are likely to arise.\n4.6 Use bundles and upsells only when they add value\nThe demonstration's revised page includes a bundle consisting of a leash, matching harness, and treat pouch. It also shows a free-shipping threshold and a satisfaction guarantee.",
      "These elements illustrate ways to increase perceived value and average order value, but they must be commercially and operationally sound.",
