  {
    "id": "chapter-7",
    "number": 7,
    "title": "Use AI to Produce and Improve Marketing Creative",
    "paragraphs": [
      "7.1 Combine strategic copy with visual execution\nThe demonstration combines a copywriting tool with an image-generation tool to create an advertisement. The underlying workflow is:\n1. Define the audience and the message.\n2. Generate copy based on the customer avatar and brand voice.\n3. Choose a visual concept that supports the message.\n4. Generate or source the creative asset.\n5. Check that the image accurately represents the product.\n6. Review the final ad as a customer would see it.\n7. Test variations and observe performance.\nThe visual should reinforce the message, not merely look attractive.\n7.2 Build creative around a single customer insight\nBefore creating an ad, write down:\n- Who is the ad for?\n- What situation will they recognise?\n- What frustration or desire does it address?\n- What is the one message they should remember?\n- What evidence or demonstration supports the claim?\n- What action should they take?\nAvoid trying to communicate every product feature in one creative. Different ads can test different customer problems or use cases.\n7.3 Create variations with a reason\nA useful testing set changes one major angle at a time, such as:\n- Problem-focused versus outcome-focused.\n- Product demonstration versus lifestyle image.\n- Direct explanation versus curiosity hook.\n- Testimonial-led versus feature-led.\n- Different customer use cases.\nKeep the offer and destination page aligned with the ad. If a specific ad promises a particular benefit, the landing page should make that benefit easy to find.\n7.4 Review AI-generated visuals carefully\nGenerated images can contain visual inaccuracies. For a physical product, inspect:\n- Product shape and proportions.\n- How the product is worn or used.\n- Materials and components.\n- Branding and logo placement.\n- Human anatomy and hand placement.\n- Text and labels.\n- Whether the image implies a feature the product does not have.\nDo not use a beautiful but misleading image. The customer should receive what the advertisement represents."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-8",
    "number": 8,
    "title": "Choose a Domain and Protect Trust",
    "paragraphs": [
      "8.1 A domain is part of the first impression\nThe source argues that a confusing or awkward domain can undermine the professional impression created by a good website.",
      "A useful domain should be:\n- Easy to read.\n- Easy to remember.\n- Easy to communicate verbally.\n- Consistent with the brand.\n- Free of confusing spelling or unnecessary punctuation.\n- Appropriate for the intended audience and market.\nA short, memorable domain is generally preferable to a long string of words and numbers.\n8.2 Do not choose a domain only for a promotional claim\nThe transcript includes a sponsored discussion of a particular domain extension and makes claims about adoption and search volume. Those promotional claims are not necessary to the business-building method and are not treated here as verified facts.",
      "The practical lesson is to compare domain options on their merits:\n- Does the extension suit the business?\n- Will customers trust and recognise it?\n- Is the name easy to type?\n- Is the renewal price acceptable?\n- Are there restrictions or renewal conditions?\n- Does it create confusion with another brand?\n- Is the domain suitable for the markets being targeted?\nA domain extension by itself does not guarantee search rankings or an SEO advantage. Search visibility depends on many factors beyond the extension.\n8.3 Check the full cost, not just the first-year offer\nBefore purchasing, examine:\n- First-year registration cost.\n- Renewal cost.\n- Premium-domain fees.\n- Privacy or add-on fees.\n- Transfer conditions.\n- Ownership and account access.\n- Whether the domain is registered in the business's control.\nPractical output: A readable, memorable domain selected after checking cost, availability, and brand suitability."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-9",
    "number": 9,
    "title": "Respond to Customers Quickly and Accurately",
    "paragraphs": [
      "9.1 Response speed is part of the sales experience\nThe source argues that the time it takes to answer a potential customer can affect revenue. A customer who has a question may be close to purchasing, but a delayed or missing response can interrupt the decision.",
      "Businesses cannot reasonably keep a person online at all hours. AI-supported customer service can help handle routine questions when the team is unavailable.",
      "The important principle is not “automate every conversation.” It is to reduce avoidable delays while ensuring customers receive accurate answers.\n9.2 Build a reliable knowledge base first\nThe demonstration uses Tidio as a website chat tool connected to a knowledge base. The example knowledge base includes:\n- Return policy.\n- Shipping times.\n- Product details.\n- Frequently asked questions.\nA chatbot is only as reliable as the information it can access. Before enabling it, prepare a clear source of truth.",
      "A useful knowledge base should include:\nProduct information\n- Product specifications.\n- Sizes, variants, and compatibility.\n- Materials and care instructions.\n- What is included in the package.\n- Known limitations.\nOrdering and fulfilment\n- Order processing times.\n- Shipping regions and estimates.\n- Tracking instructions.\n- Delivery exceptions.\n- How customers can contact the business.\nPolicies\n- Returns and exchanges.\n- Refund eligibility.\n- Warranty terms.\n- Cancellation rules.\n- Privacy and data-handling information.\nCommon questions\n- Product fit and compatibility.\n- How to use the product.\n- Differences between variants.\n- Bundle contents.\n- Payment and checkout questions.\nOnly include policies that the business has actually adopted and can honour.\n9.3 Define what the chatbot may and may not do\nA customer-service assistant can be configured to:\n- Answer common product questions.\n- Explain published policies.\n- Direct customers to relevant pages.\n- Help customers compare variants.\n- Collect details needed for a human follow-up.\n- Escalate complaints or unusual cases.\nIt should not invent:\n- Stock availability.\n- Delivery dates.\n- Refund approvals.\n- Product compatibility.\n- Warranty coverage.\n- Discounts.\n- Medical, safety, or technical assurances that have not been verified.\nFor a question such as whether a leash supports a particular dog size, the chatbot should answer only if the product specifications and testing support the answer. If the information is missing, it should say so and offer a human response.\n9.4 Create escalation rules\nHuman review is important when:\n- The customer reports a safety issue.\n- The product may be defective.\n- A refund or exception is requested.\n- The question involves a disputed payment.\n- The answer is not in the knowledge base.\n- The customer is angry or distressed.\n- The issue has legal, privacy, or other significant implications.\nTrack questions the chatbot cannot answer. These reveal gaps in product documentation, website content, or the customer experience.\n9.5 Measure service quality\nUseful measures include:\n- First-response time.\n- Time to resolution.\n- Percentage of enquiries resolved without a human.\n- Escalation rate.\n- Customer satisfaction.\n- Conversion rate among customers who use chat.\n- Repeated questions that indicate unclear website information.\nDo not optimise only for automation rate. A chatbot that answers quickly but incorrectly can damage trust.",
      "Practical output: A verified knowledge base, clear chatbot boundaries, escalation rules, and a process for reviewing unanswered questions."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-10",
    "number": 10,
    "title": "Launch, Learn, and Improve",
    "paragraphs": [
      "10.1 A first order is evidence, not proof of a durable business\nThe demonstration ends with a first order after the product, brand, website, domain, advertising creative, and customer-support process have been assembled.",
      "A first sale is an encouraging signal. It shows that at least one customer was willing to purchase under the conditions of that test. It does not prove that the business is profitable, repeatable, or scalable.",
      "The transcript does not provide enough detail to calculate the example business's full unit economics, customer acquisition cost, refund rate, fulfilment cost, or long-term retention. Those figures should not be inferred.\n10.2 Measure the whole funnel\nTrack the journey from attention to completed order.\nStage\tExample measure\tWhat it helps diagnose\nAdvertising\tImpressions and reach\tWhether the creative is being delivered\nAttention\tClick-through rate\tWhether the message earns interest\nWebsite\tLanding-page engagement\tWhether visitors understand and explore the offer\nIntent\tAdd-to-cart or checkout initiation\tWhether the offer motivates action\nPurchase\tConversion rate and orders\tWhether visitors complete the purchase\nEconomics\tRevenue, gross margin, acquisition cost\tWhether the sales are commercially viable\nService\tResponse time, refunds, complaints\tWhether the experience meets expectations",
      "Do not interpret a single metric in isolation. For example, a high click-through rate may attract poorly matched visitors. A high conversion rate may still produce losses if acquisition and fulfilment costs are too high.\n10.3 Calculate the economics before increasing spend\nFor a physical-product business, track at least:\n- Selling price.\n- Product or manufacturing cost.\n- Packaging.\n- Shipping and fulfilment.\n- Payment processing fees.\n- Discounts.\n- Returns and refunds.\n- Advertising spend.\n- Customer support and operating costs.\nA simplified contribution calculation is:\n\\[\n\\text{Contribution per order}\n=\n\\text{Net revenue}\n-\n\\text{Variable costs per order}\n\\]\nWhere variable costs may include product cost, fulfilment, payment fees, shipping subsidies, and expected return costs.",
      "Then:\n\\[\n\\text{Contribution after acquisition}\n=\n\\text{Contribution per order}\n-\n\\text{Customer acquisition cost}\n\\]\nThis is not the same as net profit. Fixed costs, taxes, owner compensation, software, overhead, and other expenses may still need to be deducted.",
      "Before scaling, establish whether each additional order contributes positively after the costs directly associated with acquiring and fulfilling it.\n10.4 Improve the weakest part of the system\nUse evidence to decide what to fix next.\n- If people do not click, test the audience, hook, or creative.\n- If people click but leave quickly, examine message-to-page consistency and page clarity.\n- If people view the product but do not add it to cart, review the offer, price, product information, and trust.\n- If people begin checkout but do not purchase, examine shipping costs, payment options, checkout friction, and unexpected conditions.\n- If customers buy but complain, investigate product quality, fulfilment, expectation-setting, or support.\n- If sales occur but margins are poor, review pricing, costs, acquisition, and the product mix.\nDo not rebuild everything at once. Identify the most likely bottleneck, make a focused change, and measure the result.\n10.5 Treat AI as a force multiplier, not a substitute for judgement\nThe final principle of the source is that AI is not a replacement for thinking. The tools in the demonstration required a strategy and a framework, and their first outputs were not necessarily good.",
      "Use AI to:\n- Reduce repetitive work.\n- Explore options.\n- Organise research.\n- Draft and revise content.\n- Generate creative directions.\n- Build initial website structures.\n- Prepare customer-support answers.\n- Increase the speed of iteration.\nRetain human responsibility for:\n- Choosing the market and offer.\n- Validating evidence.\n- Understanding customers.\n- Checking claims and calculations.\n- Approving brand and creative decisions.\n- Ensuring legal and policy compliance.\n- Handling sensitive customer issues.\n- Deciding whether the economics justify further investment.\nThe advantage comes from combining faster execution with better decisions."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-11",
    "number": 11,
    "title": "The End-to-End AI Business-Building Workflow",
    "paragraphs": [
      "The following sequence turns the playbook into an actionable process. It is a practical synthesis of the source's frameworks and demonstrations.\nPhase 1: Opportunity discovery\n1. Generate several business or product candidates.\n2. Use trend research to identify signals of demand.\n3. Check search interest, social activity, and purchase evidence.\n4. Investigate why people are buying.\n5. Apply the RRT test: Recession resistance, Raise prices, Technology.\n6. Estimate costs, competition, and operational complexity.\n7. Shortlist one opportunity for validation.\nDeliverable: Opportunity brief with evidence, risks, and RRT assessment.\nPhase 2: Customer research\n1. Identify the likely customer segments.\n2. Use a research tool to gather cited evidence.\n3. Create a first-draft avatar.\n4. Use a reasoning model to challenge unsupported assumptions.\n5. Identify the most important unknowns.\n6. Speak to potential customers or examine genuine customer discussions.\n7. Refine the avatar.\nDeliverable: Customer avatar, evidence notes, and validation questions.\nPhase 3: Offer design\n1. Describe the product or service accurately.\n2. Identify the customer's main frustration.\n3. Connect features to benefits.\n4. Define the desired outcome.\n5. Explain why the offer is meaningfully different.\n6. Decide what proof and reassurance can be provided.\n7. Check whether the offer can be delivered profitably.\nDeliverable: Offer statement and product-benefit map.\nPhase 4: Brand development\n1. Write the Villain, Victim, and Vow.\n2. Choose a brand position and personality.\n3. Generate and evaluate name ideas.\n4. Check legal and domain availability.\n5. Develop logo concepts using a detailed prompt.\n6. Select and refine a direction.\n7. Create a brand style guide.\nDeliverable: Brand brief, name shortlist, logo direction, and style guide.\nPhase 5: Website and conversion\n1. Supply the website builder with the avatar, offer, and brand guide.\n2. Generate the first website version.\n3. Rewrite the headline and subheadline for clarity.\n4. Ensure the call to action appears above the fold.\n5. Add accurate product information and visuals.\n6. Add genuine proof or transparent alternatives for a new business.\n7. Add shipping, return, and guarantee details that the business can honour.\n8. Build FAQs around real customer objections.\n9. Test the site on mobile and desktop.\n10. Check the entire purchase process.\nDeliverable: Functional, reviewed landing page and checkout journey.\nPhase 6: Marketing\n1. Separate cold and warm audiences.\n2. Develop cold-traffic hooks around genuine customer problems.\n3. Write warm-traffic copy that explains the offer and answers objections.\n4. Generate copy using the avatar and brand guide.\n5. Produce creative assets that accurately represent the product.\n6. Create a small set of distinct variations.\n7. Ensure every ad leads to a relevant page.\n8. Measure performance and improve the weakest stage.\nDeliverable: Initial campaign creative and copy variations.\nPhase 7: Customer service and operations\n1. Document product details and policies.\n2. Build a verified FAQ and knowledge base.\n3. Configure the website chat assistant.\n4. Define escalation rules.\n5. Test common questions and edge cases.\n6. Monitor response quality and unresolved enquiries.\n7. Review customer feedback for product and website improvements.\nDeliverable: Customer-support knowledge base and service workflow.\nPhase 8: Launch and validation\n1. Launch to a defined audience or limited market.\n2. Monitor visits, enquiries, carts, and orders.\n3. Record all variable costs.\n4. Calculate contribution per order and acquisition cost.\n5. Review refunds, complaints, and customer feedback.\n6. Identify the main bottleneck.\n7. Make focused improvements.\n8. Expand only when demand, delivery, and economics support it.\nDeliverable: A launch report showing what happened, what it cost, what was learned, and what should happen next."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "chapter-12",
    "number": 12,
    "title": "Common Mistakes and How to Avoid Them",
    "paragraphs": [
      "12.1 Choosing an idea before checking demand\nWhy it fails: The founder invests in a solution without knowing whether enough customers want it.",
      "Better approach: Start with market signals and customer conversations. Treat trends as leads for validation, not proof.\n12.2 Treating trend data as a business plan\nWhy it fails: High interest or sales volume does not reveal your costs, competition, margins, or ability to acquire customers.",
      "Better approach: Investigate unit economics, sourcing, fulfilment, returns, differentiation, and acquisition before committing.\n12.3 Targeting a vague audience\nWhy it fails: Generic messaging does not reflect the specific situations and motivations that influence a purchase.",
      "Better approach: Build a focused avatar and validate it against real evidence.\n12.4 Trusting AI-generated research without checking it\nWhy it fails: Models can produce unsupported claims, stale figures, or plausible but inaccurate customer details.",
      "Better approach: Check cited sources, label assumptions, and use customer conversations to resolve important unknowns.\n12.5 Publishing the first AI-generated website\nWhy it fails: The page may look complete while the offer, headline, proof, and customer relevance remain weak.",
      "Better approach: Review the page against the above-the-fold, clarity, social-proof, and customer-recognition principles.\n12.6 Using confusing or clever copy\nWhy it fails: The visitor cannot quickly understand what is being sold or why it matters.",
      "Better approach: Use the customer's language, lead with the problem, and apply the loud-bar test.\n12.7 Inventing reviews, customer counts, or guarantees\nWhy it fails: Fabricated proof misleads customers and can create legal, reputational, and platform risks.",
      "Better approach: Use only genuine, permissioned, verifiable proof. If the business is new, be transparent and build trust through accurate product information and policies.\n12.8 Designing for the founder instead of the customer\nWhy it fails: A visually attractive brand may still feel irrelevant to the intended buyer.",
      "Better approach: Use the avatar and Villain, Victim, and Vow to guide the visual identity and messaging.\n12.9 Treating a domain extension as an SEO shortcut\nWhy it fails: A domain choice cannot compensate for weak content, poor relevance, or a bad customer experience.",
      "Better approach: Choose a readable and credible domain, compare renewal costs, and build search visibility through the broader quality of the website and business.\n12.10 Automating customer service without a reliable source of truth\nWhy it fails: The chatbot may confidently provide incorrect answers about product fit, delivery, returns, or refunds.",
      "Better approach: Build and maintain a verified knowledge base, test likely questions, and escalate uncertainty.\n12.11 Scaling after one sale\nWhy it fails: A single purchase does not prove repeatable demand or profitable acquisition.",
      "Better approach: Track the funnel, calculate contribution, monitor customer experience, and validate the process over multiple orders.\n12.12 Confusing AI activity with business progress\nWhy it fails: Generating more logos, pages, prompts, and ads can feel productive without resolving the real business problem.",
      "Better approach: Tie every tool task to a business question or measurable outcome. Use AI to accelerate decisions and execution, not to create activity for its own sake."
    ]
  }
